  "description": "POV: you tap your debit card for a coffee. Follow the fee from the shop to the bank.\n\nSources:\n- Federal Reserve, Regulation II: Debit Card Interchange Fees and Routing (2023 data): https://www.federalreserve.gov/paymentsystems/2023-interchange-fee.htm\n\nFrom the cited page: in 2023, payment card networks processed 100.7 billion debit and general-use prepaid card transactions worth $4.7 trillion; interchange fees totaled $34.12 billion, an average of $0.34 per transaction; Regulation II caps a covered issuer's interchange fee at $0.21 plus 5 basis points of the transaction value (plus $0.01 if it meets fraud-prevention standards). Our math: a 5 dollar coffee x 0.05% = 0.25 cents, so 21 + 0.25 = 21.25 cents at most (before the 1 cent fraud adjustment), leaving about $4.79 for the shop; 100.7 billion x $0.34 = about $34 billion; $34.12 billion / 365 = about $93.5 million a day; / 31.5 million seconds = about $1,080 a second. The 5 dollar coffee is a made-up example; the shop pays the fee, and smaller banks that are exempt from the cap charge more. Not financial advice.\n\n#shorts #debitcard #howtheymakemoney",
